<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albert IV, Count of Tyrol</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1253">1253</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albert IV was the last Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> of the original line, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol-castle">Tyrol Castle</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merano">Merano</a>. As advocate of the bishoprics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brixen">Brixen</a> he greatly extended his power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adige">Adige</a> and Inn valleys. Having no sons, he left his lands on his death in 1253 to his sons-in-law, among them Meinhard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a>, whose son Meinhard II built a unified Tyrol.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-iv-count-of-tyrol.jpg" alt="Albert IV, Count of Tyrol" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blason_Comtes_de_Tyrol.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Albert was born around 1180, the son of Count Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and Agnes of Wangen; he is numbered Albert III in some counting schemes. He was still a minor when his father died on 14 June 1190 and began to rule independently only in 1202. From his father he inherited the office of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vogt">Vogt</a> of the bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a>, and in 1210 Bishop Conrad also appointed him Vogt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brixen">Brixen</a>. Around 1211 he married Uta, daughter of the Bavarian count Henry II of Frontenhausen-Lechsgemünd, and they had two daughters, Adelaide and Elisabeth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the Counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-andechs">Andechs-Merania</a> were banned in 1209 for their alleged role in the murder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-swabia">Philip of Swabia</a>, Albert took over their rights in the Inn, Wipp, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisack">Eisack</a> and Gader valleys. He secured his gains by recognising the Welf emperor Otto IV, and after Otto's death in 1218 he became a loyal supporter of Frederick II; in 1217 he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-crusade">Fifth Crusade</a> with Bishop Bertold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brixen">Brixen</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a> deposed Frederick in 1245, Albert stayed with the imperial party and invaded the lands of Bishop-elect Philip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1252 Philip and his father, Duke Bernard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, captured Albert and his son-in-law Meinhard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a> at Greifenburg. They were released in December after ceding possessions in Upper Carinthia, paying a large ransom and giving Meinhard's two sons as hostages. Albert died on 22 July 1253 and was buried at Stams. Having no sons, he had married his daughters to Duke Otto II of Merania and to Meinhard under mutual inheritance contracts, and on Otto's death in 1248 he gained the Merania lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and seized those of the extinct counts of Eppan.</p><p>His inheritance was first divided between Meinhard and Count Gebhard IV of Hirschberg, the second husband of Elisabeth. Gebhard had no children, so after his death Meinhard's son Meinhard II reunited Albert's possessions, which Albert had tried to join into a single county; in 1254 the whole was called the dominium Tyrolis.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Albert IV of Tyrol” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Albert IV</b>, the last of this line, left his lands in 1253 to the counts of Gorizia; Meinhard II built a unified territory from Merano to the Inn valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-tyrol">County of Tyrol</a></li></ul></section>
